#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548161} :activity/id #uuid "108aa01a-3294-597c-9fb3-d2b3890b1223" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048837 :topic/id #uuid "c4d057b8-12ac-5f2a-94f7-d0b239050514" :topic/math-academy-id 3560} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548100 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548015 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7e249d-f82c-53b4-babb-736db15aabc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547349}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547349} :answer-field/id #uuid "6916cdad-f8c8-54c9-9409-23eda9cbde44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7026529b-9d4a-5618-98a8-c1bbd9c1f097" :question/math-academy-id "q-351689" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+3)^{n}}{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}=\\frac{x+3}{2}+\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{2}^{4}}+\\frac{(x+3)^{3}}{{2}^{9}}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x+3)^{n}}{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{(x+3)^{n+1}}{{2}^{(n+1)^{2}}}}{\\frac{(x+3)^{n}}{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x+3)^{n+1}}{{2}^{(n+1)^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{{2}^{{n}^{2}}}{(x+3)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{x+3}{{2}^{(n+1)^{2}-{n}^{2}}}| \\\\  & =|x+3|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{2}^{2n+1}} \\\\  & =|x+3|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$"} :step/id #uuid "4d98d8dd-ecac-5ab1-ab66-5e749ba1a4c2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548110}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548110 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87e55e4a-4466-5d80-9cf0-611b2f8ca54b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547610}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547610} :answer-field/id #uuid "7010f27e-2c7b-56b4-bedc-53b6189ca1dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "598873cf-7c1c-5ce4-9b98-2bbec96c77b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-362919" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{n!}=(x-2)+\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{(x-2)^{3}}{3!}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{n!}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{\\frac{(x-2)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}}{\\frac{(x-2)^{n}}{n!}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-2)^{n+1}}{(n+1)!}\\cdot \\frac{n!}{(x-2)^{n}}| \\\\  & =|x-2|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{n+1} \\\\  & =|x-2|\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$  Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$"} :step/id #uuid "60eafd7c-63b8-5970-8d56-5704b401f6d8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237977 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbe55739-eaf9-56ed-9d81-8e52c2ca8a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461249} {:answer/id #uuid "529614bd-8f87-5f2c-80b4-5c37e138c066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461250}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461249} :answer-field/id #uuid "c392bd2e-f2f9-5ceb-b0b1-f3a1b122ab52" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462378} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54c50d44-335d-5651-ba10-b49f0f37fe9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461251} {:answer/id #uuid "61ae86b3-caf9-536d-bef5-f22c8cc87cac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461252}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461252} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce112048-db7b-58d9-ac72-bb8feec404bb" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462379} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26ec3d05-89ca-5e41-af7d-d0230d5cab2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461253} {:answer/id #uuid "ace51aeb-e1da-564d-aaa1-10285b642108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461254}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186461253} :answer-field/id #uuid "72accc2e-3370-511e-9b00-a598c293557b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "76850d0b-0e5d-5926-80f5-97253aff4b0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-103170" :question/problem "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x+7)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-3,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n- **I.** The series converges at $x=-11$\n- **II.** The series converges at $x=-8$\n- **III.** The series converges at $x=1$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=-7,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-3,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $4,$ i.e., $R\\ge 4.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $(-11,-3].$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $(-11,-3].$\n\n![](images/47/4764256e5f00e7a11d688bd99d5fa1c22db819e7a188113f0ef4cd987a9b7595.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $-11\\notin (-11,-3],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-11.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $-8\\in (-11,-3],$ the series is convergent at $x=-8.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $1\\notin (-11,-3],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "69ab9d00-6615-57e8-8873-36e4356ca3d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548100}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548161 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548040 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f47684-815b-5375-a6da-f93067585187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547367} {:answer/id #uuid "3a63ba58-5aa1-58f4-babf-82edf1156231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547441} {:answer/id #uuid "5707f823-a702-5205-912d-685b05a414e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547510} {:answer/id #uuid "996d9678-9618-532e-b9f5-e13ce2363d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547651} {:answer/id #uuid "9aeec267-8402-5442-ae4f-c5243381be54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547654}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547651} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c0f0b50-59c3-55f1-9dd4-e437042f4694" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a84f0335-631a-5413-a947-37ccb6b7bf94" :question/math-academy-id "q-10951" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n+2}\\,{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}=\\frac{1}{2}+(x+\\frac{1}{4})+\\frac{{3}^{2}}{4}\\,{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{{3}^{3}}{5}\\,{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{3}+⋯\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n+2}\\,{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}.$Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{3}^{n+1}{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n+1}}{n+3}\\cdot \\frac{n+2}{{3}^{n}{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{3(n+2)}{n+3}(x+\\frac{1}{4})| \\\\  & =|x+\\frac{1}{4}|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3(n+2)}{n+3} \\\\  & =|x+\\frac{1}{4}|\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3|x+\\frac{1}{4}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x+\\frac{1}{4}|<\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$"} :step/id #uuid "c7ccc968-e078-5fa8-9279-25f8596d951c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548179}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548179 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548009 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08fb89eb-ead7-518e-9d10-59dc60a26086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547336} {:answer/id #uuid "400b64b5-16b4-5ca0-8249-0be7bc1b3334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547457} {:answer/id #uuid "4636f52c-61af-5bc5-b912-63b4c6e59780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547472} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6824a2-c62e-592d-b740-ce8ac0471c2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547482} {:answer/id #uuid "879de2ba-363d-5019-bbeb-cb1ed785abbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547609}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547609} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed2165e7-e994-5fc0-830d-7875786845b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547961}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "654aa15f-b5e0-5714-95bd-cdd476eb0257" :question/math-academy-id "q-50872" :question/problem "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-1)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-3,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\nI. The series converges at $x=5$\n\nII. The series diverges at $x=-4$\n\nIII. The series converges at $x=-2$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=1,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-3,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $4,$ i.e., $R\\ge 4.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-3,5).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-3,5).$\n\n![](images/1f/1f6d98b7983946bf60df9bb29bc527af17a63a38834457404c5284b19165e7d5.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $5\\notin [-3,5),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=5.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $-4\\notin [-3,5),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is divergent at $x=-4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since $-2\\in [-3,5),$ the series is convergent at $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "e50be512-91d0-596f-b158-f62b5f78c06e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548116}}] :activity/title "Radius of Convergence of Power Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186547293}]}
